 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p>But Keisha went anyway. She was like that — gentle in appearance but stubborn underneath, the kind of stubborn that waits until no one is watching and then quietly does the thing that was forbidden. She and Delores never discussed Keisha's mother's rules. They simply did not apply to the dark, because the dark did not recognize them. The dark had its own governance, and it was looser and stranger and, they both believed, more fair.</p>
<p>They talked about leaving, sometimes. Not the neighborhood — they could not imagine leaving the neighborhood — but the circumstances. They talked about the lives they would have when they were old enough to choose, and those lives were always the same life, shared, with a house somewhere far from here and a garden and a porch and no lights at all on the street outside. They would sit on that porch — they could see it perfectly, even then — and they would talk the way they were talking now, in the orange light, in the cold air, with no one watching and nothing to prove. Keisha would have a dog. Delores would learn to cook something real. The dark would come every night and it would be the same dark they knew, the dark that was theirs.</p>
<p>They were eleven. They did not know yet that the place you imagine is not the place you get, and that the future is not a destination but a series of detours that look, from the inside, like the only possible road.</p>
<p>The first streetlight was installed on the corner of Elm and 4th, outside Mrs. Carter's house. It took four months for the rest to follow. Delores watched them come one by one, like something being stitched into the sky, and each time she felt the neighborhood contract — the porch lights go out earlier, the voices stop sooner, the rhythm of the evening shift toward something she did not recognize.</p>
<p>Keisha noticed it too. They used to meet at the corner of Maple and 5th, at the telephone pole with the single bulb, and talk for an hour before going home. After the lights came, they stopped meeting. Not because they decided to stop — because the place where they used to meet stopped existing. The corner was still there. The pole was still there. But the thing that had made it a place — the dark that held it — was gone.</p>
<p>You could see everything now. That was the problem. You could see, which meant everyone else could see you, which meant no one was being themselves anymore. They were being seen. There is a difference. Delores felt it that first winter, when she walked to Keisha's house and found her sitting on the porch with the television on and the door open and a look on her face that said she had forgotten what the porch was for.</p>
<p>The dark had a purpose. The light just showed you what you'd already decided was there.</p>
<p>Keisha did not talk the way she used to talk after the lights came. Not because she had changed — she was still Keisha, still stubborn and soft-footed and brave in the specific way that meant doing the thing that was forbidden when no one was watching — but because there was no place left where talking felt safe. The new lights made the porches visible from the street, and the streets visible from the porches, and what had been private was now a matter of geography, and the geography had changed, and Keisha was a different person in the new geography. Delores understood this. She was a different person too. They were still friends — they would be friends for another six years, through high school, through the years when Keisha's mother finally succeeded in moving them to a town two hours north where the streetlights had been there from the beginning — but they were not the people they had been in the dark, and neither of them knew how to talk about that.</p>
<p>Keisha left in March of 1993. She did not say goodbye in the usual way. She called at seven in the evening, which was the old time, the time when they would have been walking, and said: my mother says we are moving. I wanted to tell you first. Delores stood in her own hallway with the phone cord wrapped around her fingers and listened to the voice that she had heard in the orange light say the thing that was true — I will miss the walking — and she said it back, and they did not cry, because crying was something you did when you were alone, and they were not alone yet, not quite, not until the line went quiet.</p>
<p>Delores is fifty-three now. She lives in a different neighborhood, one that was built after the streetlight era and carries none of its history. She thinks about the old neighborhood sometimes — not with nostalgia, exactly, but with the feeling you get when you realize you've lost something and can't remember what it was.</p>
<p>The other day she drove back through it. The houses are smaller than she remembered. The trees are gone. The corner of Maple and 5th has a new pole, new lights, a sign advertising a laundromat that wasn't there before. Everything is visible. Nothing is hidden. She sat at the corner for ten minutes and waited to feel what she used to feel there, but the corner didn't know her anymore, and the dark was gone, and the thing she had lost had a name after all, even if no one had ever written it down.</p>
<p>It was called being unknown. It was called belonging to the night. It was called the hours before the light arrived, when anyone could be anyone, and no one was watching, and the world was still deciding what it wanted to be.</p>
<p>She drove home in the dark, which is not the same thing as what she remembered, but is close enough to make her sad.</p>
<p>She has thought, sometimes, about writing to Keisha. The last she heard — this was years ago, through the old channels, the grapevine that runs through every neighborhood whether it has streetlights or not — Keisha was living in the same town her mother had chosen, working at a school, raising two daughters. Delores has imagined the conversation they might have, the two of them at sixty or seventy, sitting on some porch in some place that is finally quiet, finally dark enough, saying the things they never said when they were eleven and had a garden near the tracks and a circle of orange light and the whole night ahead of them and no idea that they were living in the part of their lives they would spend the rest of their lives missing.</p>
<p>She has not written. Some things, she thinks, belong to the dark. You cannot bring them into the light without changing what they are. You cannot translate them into language without losing whatever it was that made them true in the first place. Keisha would understand this. Keisha, who walked barefoot on cold concrete because the walking was better with bare feet, and who believed in the dark even after the dark was gone, and who said on the phone that last evening: I will miss the walking. And meant it. And did not know yet that missing the walking was not a feeling you recovered from, but a condition you learned to live inside, the way some people learn to live in a house where someone they loved once lived. You do not get over it. You furnish around it. You build a life in the rooms that are still yours, and you leave the dark room exactly as it was, and you do not turn on the light when you pass the door.</p>
<p>Delores still walks at night. Not in her neighborhood — the streets are too bright, the traffic too present, the world too awake in the way that cities are awake now, everywhere, all the time. She drives to a place on the outskirts, a road that runs along the edge of a field with no houses on it, and she parks with the headlights off and sits in the car with the windows down, and she waits for the dark to arrive the way it used to arrive, slow and certain, filling the air from the ground up. It is not the same dark. It is not even close. But it is dark enough to remind her that she was once a girl who walked through a neighborhood that belonged to her, and she knew every corner of it, and she knew who she was in the dark between the porch lights, and she has never since been as certain of anything as she was of that.</p>
